      "text": "To determine which functions have inverses, we must check if every point in the domain of a function maps to a unique value in the range of that function. If a function does not have this property, then it does not have an inverse.\n\nFor function A, we can see that each x-value of the domain corresponds to a unique y-value in the range. We can confirm this by trying to evaluate the function at various points in the domain. For instance, when we plug -5 into the function, we get:\n\nf(-5) = (-5)^3/40 + (-5)^2/20 - (-5)/2 + 2\nf(-5) = -134/40 + 25/20 + 5/2 + 2\n\nSimplfying these fractions, we get:\n\nf(-5) = -5.85 + 1.25 + 2.5 + 2\nf(-5) = 4.85\n\nThis checks out, so A does have an inverse.\n\nFor function B, we can see that each x-value in the domain maps to multiple y-values in the range. Specifically, for x = 0, the function gives two outputs: 2 and -2. Since a function must always map each input to a unique output, B does not have an inverse.\n\nFor function C, we are given a sketch of the graph. However, without a careful examination of the graph, we cannot determine if every x-value in the domain maps to a unique y-value. Thus, we cannot determine if C has an inverse.\n\nFor function D, we can check each x-value in the domain to see if it maps to a unique output in the range. From the graph, it appears that every x-value in the domain maps to a unique y-value in the range. Therefore, according to the conditions of an inverse function, D does have an inverse.\n\nFinally, we examine function E. Based on the graph, we can see that each x-value in the domain maps to multiple y-values in the range. For instance, when we input -5/2 into the function, we get two outputs of 4.85. Therefore, E does not have an inverse.\n\nIn conclusion, the functions that have inverses are A and D. So the solution is A, D.\nA, D",
